Description:

Operations which touch more than one user now take those users' namespace locks
in a consistent order, or one at a time, so they can't deadlock against each
other.

A user namespace lock has always been per-user, and taking two of them in
opposite orders deadlocks.  user_nslock_lockmulti() locks any number of users in
a fixed order for the cases where the whole set is known up front, and
user_nslock_lock() now logs an acquisition made in the wrong order against one
already held, naming both locks and the functions on each end.

Fixed with that: renaming a user held both users' locks while moving sharees'
subscriptions and while re-selecting; the DAV DB, the sieve helpers in
sync_server and timsieved reached into other users without a lock at all;
Principal/getAvailability read the principal's calendars under the requesting
account's lock; and iTIP delivered to attendees while still holding the
organizer's.  The DAV DBs are now tagged with the scope which protects them and
complain if it isn't held.

A Sieve "fileinto" whose target belongs to another user, or to the shared
namespace, is likewise delivered after the recipient's own delivery has
committed, and all the appends for one owner are grouped so they stay atomic
for that user.  A failure there still puts one copy in the recipient's INBOX,
as a failed fileinto always has.  Creating the target folder now locks the
namespace it is being created in rather than the recipient's.

iTIP scheduling messages are now delivered after the request which caused them
has finished with the organizer's calendar.  For CalDAV that means the
SCHEDULE-STATUS of each attendee is written to the stored resource in a second
store afterwards, so a scheduling PUT no longer returns an ETag - clients must
re-read the resource, as they already must when default alarms rewrite it.


Documentation:

doc/internal/cross-user-lock-ordering.md covers the locking rules and what is
still outstanding.


